# The alignment canary, across two allocations rather than one struct.
#
# `Producer::on_distinct_lines` is the only one of the three that computes the
# comparison itself, because its two cursors are not fields of a shared parent:
# one lives in the ring's gating set, the other in the claimer. Its own doc says
# so, and says why it checks at all. A sibling change that dropped the padding
# would cost this crate a contended line on its hottest path and break nothing
# that compiles.
#
# Separate allocations do not weaken the conclusion. Both are `PaddedCursor`,
# both are 64-byte aligned, and two distinct addresses that are each a multiple
# of 64 differ by at least 64 wherever they were allocated. The method is
# unconditionally true, and unkillable while `align_of::< PaddedCursor >()` is
# what its own crate asserts it to be.
#
# See accepted/readme.md, "The alignment canaries".
#
# NOT accepted, and deliberately absent: this crate's `Consumer::is_empty` and
# `Ring::committed`, both real one-polarity defects found in the same sweep and
# both now held by `mutant/S4` and `mutant/S11`.

ring_mpsc/src/lib.rs:996:5: replace Producer<'a, S>::on_distinct_lines -> bool with true

# `ReservedBatch::is_empty` returning `false` unconditionally, which it already
# does.
#
# `Producer::claim_batch` is the only way to get a `ReservedBatch`, and it wraps
# whatever `ring_claim::Claimer::claim_up_to` grants. That grant is at least one
# sequence: a headroom or a `max` of zero exits to `Full` instead of granting
# nothing. So every `ReservedBatch` that can exist is non-empty, and a mutant
# returning `false` returns the same answer. An equivalent mutant, the same kind
# as `ring_store`'s `Buffer::is_empty`.
#
# The other polarity, returning `true`, is a real defect and is killed by
# `a_batch_claim_grants_what_headroom_allows_rather_than_the_full_ask`.

ring_mpsc/src/lib.rs:1241:5: replace ReservedBatch<'_, S>::is_empty -> bool with false
